4
00:00:14.532 --> 00:00:22.621
(narrator) A vintage 1960s mainframe has two large tape reels and a small enlarged cutaway of its core memory: rings threaded on crossing wires.

5
00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:25.905
(narrator) Memory was expensive: dollar bills pile up beneath it.

6
00:00:25.905 --> 00:00:32.291
(narrator) Split-flap digits for 1985 lose their first two digits, only 85 is stored, and the money pile drops away.

7
00:00:32.291 --> 00:00:43.482
(narrator) A two-digit field rolls from 99 to 00, and a green-phosphor terminal shows the computer reading 19 plus 00 as 1900, and a desk calendar tears from December 31, 1999 to January 1, 1900.

8
00:00:43.482 --> 00:00:55.281
(narrator) Another terminal file computes an age as 00 minus 85, which is minus 85 years old, while a young man born in 85 rewinds into a teen, a kid, a baby, and then a puff of smoke with a question mark.

9
00:00:55.281 --> 00:01:05.195
(narrator) Twelve case files, from payroll to grades, all carry the same red YY secret tag and turn red; an intercept console searches for YY and finds it in file after file.

10
00:01:05.195 --> 00:01:19.305
(narrator) A mission clock turns over to January 1, 2000, celebratory fireworks bloom around it, almost nothing breaks, and red dollar bills flood the whole frame behind a classified expense sheet that totals billions of dollars, stamped paid.

11
00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:26.421
(narrator) A case file gives the title, Every module should keep a secret, stamped top secret, while your hero sits at a laptop.

12
00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:31.333
